**Key idea:** Let $Q(x)=xP(x)$, an even-degree polynomial. For large $|x|$, $Q$ is strictly monotone on each side, so any $y\neq x$ with $Q(y)=Q(x)$ and $x$ large must lie on the *opposite* side. Setting $y=-z$ reduces $Q(x)=Q(-z)$ to $xP(x)=-zP(-z)$. Since both sides are even-degree polynomials in their arguments with the *same* leading coefficient, $x-z$ is bounded; by pigeonhole it is a constant $k$ for infinitely many pairs, forcing a polynomial identity $(t+k)P(t+k)=-tP(-t)$. Plugging $t=0$ gives $kP(k)=0$, yielding either $P(0)=0$ (when $k=0$) or $P(k)=0$ (when $k\neq 0$).

